# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# What must pass before main takes a merge. github-guard reads this from the
# default branch on the server and applies it to branch protection.
#
#   ci-ok   ci.yml: the one always-run gate. It `needs:` every other job and
#           fails when any of them failed, was cancelled or was SKIPPED: the
#           unit tier with no fixtures, the kernel-made fixtures built in the
#           fs-linux-test-harness VM, the whole suite with every xfsprogs call
#           and every kernel mount inside that VM (x86_64), the tiers that
#           need no VM on aarch64 Linux and on aarch64 Darwin, and the suite
#           compiled and run inside the guest.
#
# WHY ONE NAME AND NOT FIVE (#207). The list used to name three real job
# names — `test-ubuntu-latest`, `test-darwin` and
# `validate against xfs_db + in-kernel XFS driver` — which meant that
# renaming a job, splitting it into a matrix leg or adding one changed what
# gates a merge without anyone deciding to. Both failure directions had
# already happened in this constellation: a job that ran on every pull
# request and gated nothing (#201, `test-darwin`), and a required context no
# job produces, which GitHub reads as permanently pending with enforce_admins
# on and no failure to point at.
#
# ORDERING, BECAUSE THIS IS A CHICKEN AND EGG. github-guard reads this file
# from the DEFAULT BRANCH, so the pull request that renames the jobs cannot
# also be the one whose protection knows the new name — the old names are
# still required while it is open, and they no longer report. The sequence
# that works is: merge with the old names still required and
# `validate against xfs_db + in-kernel XFS driver` failing to report, then
# re-run github-guard against main, which reads this file and requires
# `ci-ok` alone. Admin merge of this one pull request is the cost of the
# change; every later one is gated by a name that survives a rename.
#
# The name must match the check-run name GitHub reports, which here is the
# job's `name:` rather than its key — `ci-ok` for both.
#
# WHY test-darwin GATES rather than advising. It builds the staticlib that
# DiskJockey's XFS extension links against, for aarch64-apple-darwin, and runs
# the suite on that target on an arm64 host. It is the only macOS/aarch64
# coverage in this repository, and the failures it exists to catch are exactly
# the ones no Linux job can see: the one target-conditional value in the C ABI
# (ENOTSUP is 45 on Darwin and 95 on Linux, so tests/capi.rs's ENOTSUP asserts
# were never compiled against their Darwin branch until this job existed),
# `c_char` signedness at the C ABI boundary, and pointer-width and alignment
# assumptions that x86_64 Linux happens to satisfy. A break there ships to the
# consumer that links the artifact, and `test-ubuntu-latest` stays green
# through all of it. A check that cannot block the merge it disagrees with is
# a check that reports rather than gates.
#
# Only these three: they are the only checks that run on `pull_request`.
# release.yml is `on: push: tags: ['v*']` and stress.yml is workflow_dispatch
# plus a weekly cron, so neither reports on a pull request at all, and a
# required check that never reports is a permanent block on every merge —
# GitHub reads "required and absent" as pending, with enforce_admins on and
# no failure to point at.
#
# ONE NAME, NOW THAT THERE IS A JOB THAT STANDS FOR THE REST (#207).
#
# `ci-ok` in ci.yml `needs:` every other job and fails unless each concluded
# success, with `if: always()` so a cancelled or skipped job is still judged.
# Requiring it alone is what survives the next rename, matrix split or added
# job: those change what `ci-ok` waits on, in the same diff that makes them,
# rather than changing what a settings page says days later.
#
# tests/ci_aggregate_gate.rs holds both halves to it — every job in ci.yml has
# to be in `ci-ok`'s `needs`, and this file has to require `ci-ok` and nothing
# else.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports, which are the `name:`
# of each job rather than its key.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
